Lua Virtual Machine Decoding – Analysis and Examples

1. Understanding the Decoding Process

The Lua script appears to use a custom Virtual Machine (VM) to process encoded instructions instead of executing ordinary Lua code directly.

The decoding process can be studied through several components.

2. Varint Decoding

Varint (Variable-Length Integer) encoding stores integers using one or more bytes.

Example Input

local bytes = {172, 2}

Example Decoder

local function decode_varint(bytes, pos)
    local result = 0
    local shift = 0

    while true do
        local b = bytes[pos]
        pos = pos + 1

        result = result + (b % 128) * (128 ^ shift)

        if b < 128 then
            return result, pos
        end

        shift = shift + 1
    end
end

local value, next_pos = decode_varint(bytes, 1)

print(value)    -- 300
print(next_pos) -- 3

This example demonstrates how multiple bytes can represent a single integer.

3. Opcode Dispatching

An opcode dispatcher determines which operation should be executed.

Example

local registers = {}

local function execute(opcode, a, b, c)
    if opcode == 1 then
        registers[a] = b + c

    elseif opcode == 2 then
        registers[a] = b * c

    elseif opcode == 3 then
        registers[a] = b
    end
end

execute(1, 1, 10, 20)

print(registers[1]) -- 30

Each opcode represents a different operation.

4. Virtual Register Operations

A Virtual Machine can use a register table to store intermediate values.

Example

local J = {}

J[1] = 10
J[2] = 20

J[3] = J[1] + J[2]

print(J[3]) -- 30

The table "J" represents a simplified virtual register system.

